2.3 Graduations of the screen



2.3.1 Statusline

In the first line you see what type of game it is. 
Possible is:

  For borders:     - NoBor. (playgroundmargins are no borders!-
                                       - continue at opposite side)
                   - Border (playgroundmargins are borders!-
                                             - no way over them )

  At Fluttype:     - Normal (flooding goes horizontal and vertical!)
                   - X-Flut (flooding goes diagonal! cross-way)



2.3.2 Playground

Beneath you see the playground.  Sorry for not colouring the bucketfillings
in this documentation.  :)

The right part of the screen is split in three sections:



2.3.3 Players

   - Name means the handle of the user.
   -  1/3,  2/3  and  3/3  means  the  fillingvalue.  Displayed is how much
          buckets of each value the user owns.
   - Last means when played the user for the last time (in days).
          When the last turn was 3.0 days ago, you can fill in twice.
   - Next means the time the user can take his next turn. You can play
          once a day exactly. But it must be min.12 hours since your
          last turn.

When the player has joined the selected game, a ">" points at his entry.



2.3.4 Gamedisplay

   "your Games" are games which concern the user directly:

   - turn   (Users turn is now)
   - in     (User is in the game, but it`s not his turn yet)
   - joined (User has joined the game, but some players are still missing)
   - end    (User has no more possibility to take his turn-everything full-)

   "other" not concerning him directly:

   - out    (User didn`t join this game)
   - wait   (User didn`t join, but he is still able to)
   - empty  (Game is empty - not startet yet - no entries yet)

The  different  game-conditions  are coloured various.  In the same colours
the concerning game-numbers are displayed for easier identifing.



2.3.5 Possible choises

   - S:elect a game     >> 04 << (you can enter the game-number)

   left side:

   - A:utoselect turns (selects the games automaticly, where you may take
                        your next turn)
   - P:oints           (Information about points-valuations is shown)
   - I:nstructions     (Short instruction is displayed)
   - W:all             (... a Wall - one for every game and one is global)
   - Q:uit back to bbs (Quit Flut! and return to CNet)

   right side:

   - C:lock            (shows actual time and the time when your next turn
                       in any game is on)
   - N:ews report      (a newspaper with the ended games on - the Date,
                       the game-number, last player and! the winner)
   - R:ankings         (actual rankinglist - name, points and number of
                        needed games)
   - H:all of Flut     (the best players - Date, number of needed games and
                        the name)(who guess?):)

   undocumented keys:

   - "+" (one game ahead)
   - "-" (one game back)

   these are only available at keyboard and
                                not for your users, cause they can:
   - maybe easy drop carrier (+++)!!!
   - not know, what background-actions like harddiskfunctions or else happen.



3. Installation

Copy  the  whole  directory "Flut" to your "Pfiles:" and copy "Megatext" to
your "c:"-DIR.

Copy  all files from  "Text_E"  to  "Pfiles:Flut" and   delete  "Text_D"  and
"Text_E".

Then adopt the file "Flut" as a CNet-C - file.

To start the server you can add the following line to your startup-sequence :
"run pfiles:flut/flut-server".
If not ,   the server  will be started  automaticly at  first calling  and is
available at the second.

Thats all !


